  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Purple-edged Lute (Callistosporium purpureomarginatum) is a delicate woodland mushroom characterized by its distinctive purplish margins and lute-shaped (spatulate) cap. This small fungus belongs to the Callistosporiaceae family and is a decomposer playing an important ecological role in New England forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Purple-tinged Margins",
      "description": "The cap edges display a distinctive purple or violet discoloration that fades toward the pale center, giving the mushroom its common name.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spatulate Cap Shape",
      "description": "The cap is characteristically lute or spoon-shaped, broader at the top and narrowing toward the stalk, distinguishing it from round-capped relatives.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Fragility",
      "description": "This species is typically small (1-3 cm across) with thin, delicate flesh that bruises easily, making careful handling essential for identification.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Purple-edged Lute fruiting bodies appear on rich forest floors and leaf litter in hardwood and mixed forests throughout New England, preferring moist conditions in summer and fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The lute-shaped cap of this fungus inspired both its scientific genus name (Callistosporium, meaning 'beautiful spore') and its evocative common name, reflecting the aesthetic appeal of this delicate woodland mushroom.",
